latest_plan = 'Based on the reflection, here\'s a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Analyze the input grid:\n   a. Identify all unique colors in the grid.\n   b. Calculate the frequency of each color.\n   c. Determine the grid dimensions.\n\n2. Identify the largest contiguous shape:\n   a. Use a flood fill algorithm to find all connected regions for each color.\n   b. Determine the largest region by cell count.\n   c. Store the color and coordinates of this largest shape.\n\n3. Create a significance map:\n   a. Identify all regions that occupy more than 5% of the grid.\n   b. Mark these regions as "significant" to be preserved.\n\n4. Generate an expansion priority map:\n   a. For each cell adjacent to the largest shape:\n      - Assign high priority (3) if it has 2 or more neighbors of the target color.\n      - Assign medium priority (2) if it has 1 neighbor of the target color.\n   b. For cells within a 2-cell radius of the shape, assign low priority (1).\n   c. Assign priority 0 to all other cells.\n\n5. Calculate initial shape characteristics:\n   a. Determine the shape\'s compactness (area / perimeter^2).\n   b. Calculate the shape\'s elongation (length of major axis / minor axis).\n   c. Use these metrics to set adaptive thresholds for expansion.\n\n6. Iterative expansion process:\n   a. Start with high priority cells (3).\n   b. For each priority level (from 3 to 1):\n      - Iterate through cells of current priority.\n      - If the cell is not part of a significant region, change its color to the target color.\n      - After each iteration, recalculate priorities for adjacent cells.\n      - Update shape characteristics.\n   c. Stop expansion if:\n      - The shape has grown by more than 20% of its original size, or\n      - No more cells of priority 2 or 3 are available, or\n      - Shape characteristics (compactness, elongation) have changed significantly.\n\n7. Smoothing pass:\n   a. Identify single-cell intrusions and fill them if not part of a significant region.\n   b. Identify single-cell protrusions:\n      - Remove if they don\'t connect to other parts of the shape.\n      - Keep if removal would disconnect parts of the shape.\n\n8. Connectivity check:\n   a. Perform a final flood fill on the expanded shape.\n   b. If any cells of the target color are not connected, revert them to their original color.\n\n9. Balance check:\n   a. Calculate the new color distribution of the grid.\n   b. If the target color has increased by more than 25% of its original coverage, revert the least important expanded cells until below this threshold.\n\n10. Final cleanup:\n    a. Ensure that corner regions (3x3 in each corner) remain unchanged if they were initially uniform.\n    b. Verify that no significant regions identified in step 3 have been overwritten.\n\n11. Generate the output grid:\n    a. Create a new ColoredGrid object with the modified values.\n    b. Return this as the solution.\n\nThis plan addresses the key aspects identified in our reflection:\n- It preserves significant secondary patterns.\n- It uses adaptive thresholds based on initial shape characteristics.\n- It balances enhancement with preservation of the original structure.\n- It includes steps to ensure connectivity and overall balance.\n- It considers both local (cell-level) and global (grid-level) characteristics in the enhancement process.\n\nBy following this plan, we should be able to generate solutions that work consistently across all provided examples and test cases, while also being flexible enough to handle new, unseen inputs.'
